  "final_critic_review_md": "## Disposition: accept\n\n**The exact candidate solves both parts of the problem under its explicitly recorded reduced-skein, generic-\\(q\\) interpretation.** I reread the entire candidate and baseline, checked the essential cited results, independently recalculated the normalization-sensitive steps, and compiled a fresh reconstruction of the supplied LaTeX.\n\nAll five corrections required by the preceding review are now resolved. I found **no lost essential argument, substantive mathematical gap, weakened conclusion, or remaining required editorial correction**.\n\nThe scope is unchanged from the baseline:\n\\[\nn\\ge2,\\qquad \\mathbb K=\\mathbb Q(q^{1/(2n)}),\\qquad q\\text{ indeterminate}.\n\\]\nNeither text purports to establish an integral-form or arbitrary-specialization theorem.\n\n## 1. Disposition of the earlier findings\n\n| Previous finding | Current candidate | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. False universal claim that the saturated-arcs multiplication map is not an algebra isomorphism** | (S2) now states only a linear isomorphism and expressly disclaims any assertion that different factors commute | **Resolved** |\n| **B. False claim that the toral factors commute with everything involved in distant-root commutation** | Lemma 4.1 now identifies exactly the required cross-factor commutations | **Resolved** |\n| **C. Missing normalization in descriptions of individual height exchanges** | (S3) and Lemma 3.1, Step 2 consistently retain \\(q^{-1/n}\\), then cancel the common factor before obtaining (29) | **Resolved** |\n| **D. Saturation argument inconsistent with the new ideal-point/puncture terminology** | Step 1 now excludes a complementary component containing neither a boundary ideal point nor an interior puncture | **Resolved** |\n| **E. Overfull paragraph in §1.2** | The sentence has been reflowed; the stable compilation reports no overfull box | **Resolved** |\n\nThe two previous presentation suggestions are also addressed: bijectivity is attributed to the established linear isomorphisms, and the quotient calculation is described as a consistency check rather than a derivation of the conventions.\n\nRemoving the `%% FLAG` comments has not concealed an unresolved dependency. The relevant coefficient conventions, matrix-basis ordering, bad-arc conventions, and exchange coefficients were rechecked independently.\n\n## 2. Full mathematical audit\n\n### A. Interpretation, assumptions, and quantum-group conventions\n\nThe interpretation section preserves the baseline’s choice of the **reduced** stated skein algebra. It fixes the missing diagram, endpoint indexing, and arc names sufficiently to make the formulas unambiguous. The bad-arc inequalities agree with the cited definition, including both orientations. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.08082v2))\n\nThe field contains the fractional powers required by the constructions actually used, and \\(s=q-q^{-1}\\) is invertible. Lê–Sikora’s coefficient convention permits the stated skein algebra over this field. No root-of-unity assumption or specialization is silently introduced. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.00045v3))\n\nThe expanded Borel presentations agree with the baseline. In particular:\n\n- the product-one toral relation is compatible with the pairing;\n- \\(\\langle E_i,F_j\\rangle=-\\delta_{ij}/s\\) is exactly the pairing in the problem;\n- the positive and negative root recursions retain their different, necessary \\(q\\)-powers;\n- the toral lattice remains the weight lattice, not merely the root lattice.\n\n### B. Proposition 2.1: presentation of the double\n\nThe crossing-rule calculations give the asserted mixed relations with the correct signs. In particular,\n\\[\n-\\frac{\\delta_{ij}}sK_i^++E_iF_j\n=\nF_jE_i-\\frac{\\delta_{ij}}s(K_i^-)^{-1}\n\\]\ngives precisely (13).\n\nThe completeness argument is valid. Its induction counts positive–negative inversions anywhere in a word; an adjacent exchange either decreases that count or produces a correction with fewer Chevalley letters.\n\nThe subsequent comparison with the defining vector space of the double proves injectivity of the presented algebra’s map to \\(\\mathcal D\\). It does not assume confluence or infer an isomorphism from surjectivity alone.\n\n### C. Lemma 3.1: the reduced skein presentation\n\nThis essential part of the baseline has been preserved.\n\n**Unreduced factorization.** Both strips join distinct boundary edges. The complementary-region argument establishes maximality as well as the one-removed-point condition. The cited saturated-arcs results therefore apply, including the multiplication-map formulation and freedom to choose boundary orders. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.00045v3))\n\n**Mixed exchange.** Step 2 starts from the appropriate mixed-height configuration, not from either ordinary product. The two expansions have the common normalization \\(q^{-1/n}\\). Their state-interchange conditions are \\(i>k\\) and \\(j<l\\), respectively, giving (29) after cancellation. These choices agree with the cited boundary-crossing and height conventions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.08082v2))\n\n**Rightward bad-arc ideal.** Equation (31) is the correct expansion of\n\\[\nC_2B_1=RB_1C_2R^{-1}.\n\\]\nEvery nonzero term preserves a forbidden entry of the relevant family. For example, its fourth term satisfies:\n\n- if \\(i<j\\), then its indicators imply \\(k<i<j<l\\), so \\(b_{kl}\\) remains bad;\n- if \\(k>l\\), they imply \\(i>k>l>j\\), so \\(c_{ij}\\) remains bad.\n\nThe argument then verifies all four multiplication directions and both ideal containments. It establishes an equality of ideals, not merely a spanning property.\n\n**Reversed bad arcs.** The antipode formula has the correct barred indices and nonzero scalar. The triangular inverse argument is legitimate over the noncommutative quotient: the commuting diagonal entries are units, and the strictly triangular part is nilpotent. Thus the opposite orientations introduce no additional ideal generators. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.08082v2))\n\n**Presentation completeness.** The quotient factorization is established before it is used to prove independence. Consequently, the proof does not assume that reduction preserves the unreduced factorization; it proves precisely that compatibility.\n\n### D. Lemma 4.1: triangular matrices and the Borels\n\nThe corrected distant-root explanation is valid. The adjacent-root calculation proves all three identities in (35), and their expansion gives **both** adjacent Serre relations.\n\nThe recursion\n\\[\n[t_{ik},t_{kj}]=s\\,t_{ij}d_k\n\\]\nproves generation and yields the stated higher-root image without an extra \\(q\\)-factor.\n\nThe two PBW inputs support their uses:\n\n- Lê–Yu Proposition 3.10 permits the required ordering in the triangular quotient; transposition supplies the upper-triangular version. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.08082v2))\n- Chen–Shao–Shum’s parameter conversion \\(v=q^{1/2}\\) gives\n  \\[\n  x_{ij}=v^{j-i-1}E_{ij},\n  \\]\n  and the indicated Gröbner–Shirshov theorem supplies the lexicographically ordered monomials. Its parameter restriction is satisfied here. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0804.0954v1))\n\nThe injectivity argument retains the crucial exponent calculation\n\\[\nr_a\\longmapsto r_a-M_a+M_n,\\qquad a<n.\n\\]\nFor fixed root exponents this is a bijective translation, while different root-exponent collections remain distinct.\n\nFinally, transposition and\n\\[\nH_a^-\\mapsto(H_a^+)^{-1},\\qquad F_i\\mapsto-q^{-1}E_i\n\\]\ngive exactly the lower-Borel normalization in (33). Only an algebra isomorphism is asserted and needed.\n\n### E. Section 5: construction and bijectivity\n\nThe specializations (38)–(41) are correct. The additional terms in the unequal-index case vanish by triangularity.\n\nThe mixed Chevalley calculation introduces no residual power of \\(q\\). It gives\n\\[\n[e_i,f